        "1": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa",
        "2": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa",
        "3": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa",
        "4": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa"
      },
      "units": [
        {
          "id": "ecs-primer-01",
          "slide": 1,
          "text": "Amazon Elastic Container Service, or E C S, runs and coordinates containers on A W S. A container packages an app with what it needs to start. E C S helps keep the number of copies you asked for running.",
          "text_sha256": "bc01dd35bc44bdc88f5a29b644c79eff153430b866afb60e6432574d91e10bdc",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "4cb4bfc604d92fd23c59e4f562f628a122276e95d8f96d3be2b4fd150878391a",
          "asset_sha256": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa"
        },
        {
          "id": "ecs-primer-02",
          "slide": 2,
          "text": "Here, an orders app needs two copies. The task definition is the recipe. It names the container image and says how much C P U and memory each copy needs. A running copy is called a task.",
          "text_sha256": "d55434bad87a1650783c3f1c3ccd5b9d0635b8b344ec191d62e3bf2622465e26",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "bc8fc4def427f9853264152fbcea623bd8f31a6b843d36f784f0b92aa95d9de1",
          "asset_sha256": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa"
        },
        {
          "id": "ecs-primer-03",
          "slide": 3,
          "text": "An E C S service asks for two tasks. If one stops, the service starts a replacement to get back to two. For a one-off job, you can run a task without asking a service to replace it.",
          "text_sha256": "3e08ccb622a9ee5d8f4b6b85fcdff2b5a5a5e34f1a652626936369d60a16f614",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "b4746572101d56f0cc961ff17a45f9daf3647f693484cb930a9bfbee7b27262c",
          "asset_sha256": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa"
        },
        {
          "id": "ecs-primer-04",
          "slide": 4,
          "text": "Choose the compute for your tasks. With A W S Fargate, you don't manage the servers. With Amazon E C two capacity, you can choose and manage those hosts yourself.",
          "text_sha256": "702dc0c8f5dce0617a5ec235f03ed9f7b4cec39b5f94a31e49afb34bb1a7c3df",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "88d00e968fb1491178c39ce170517cc5e68b2568d65cc503572e5975d8547462",
          "asset_sha256": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "module": "12",
      "part": "96",
      "revision": "m12p96-sap-m12-foundations-andrew-v1",
      "approved_assets": {
        "1": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa",
        "2": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa",
        "3": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa",
        "4": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa"
      },
      "units": [
        {
          "id": "eks-primer-01",
          "slide": 1,
          "text": "Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service, or E K S, is managed Kubernetes on A W S. Kubernetes coordinates container applications. E K S operates the control plane, the part that accepts your instructions and coordinates the work.",
          "text_sha256": "845afd149c01e86fc3b8e3c23d0c2a59619a1d13dc6697ba9ae14af9acb6c1f6",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "dcc4e3573b512a1d1736fbde309ba31fc9b58e105a160bfca292187419dd7f4e",
          "asset_sha256": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa"
        },
        {
          "id": "eks-primer-02",
          "slide": 2,
          "text": "Say your orders app needs two copies. Tell Kubernetes to run two. Each copy runs in a Pod, the unit Kubernetes schedules. The Pods use workload compute, not the control plane.",
          "text_sha256": "fd0bc9dca5cf18cb8f404f7961e075f8a4d3c9663eb92afe48ef87e9e0c9ff55",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "fd0bc9dca5cf18cb8f404f7961e075f8a4d3c9663eb92afe48ef87e9e0c9ff55",
          "asset_sha256": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa"
        },
        {
          "id": "eks-primer-03",
          "slide": 3,
          "text": "If one Pod stops or disappears, Kubernetes works to create a replacement. While that happens, the control plane can still be working even if the application has too few ready copies.",
          "text_sha256": "6059095e33588b5a99a16dabb97629adb63734465715cb9cbe9dbee658dcf4d3",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "6059095e33588b5a99a16dabb97629adb63734465715cb9cbe9dbee658dcf4d3",
          "asset_sha256": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa"
        },
        {
          "id": "eks-primer-04",
          "slide": 4,
          "text": "That distinction helps you troubleshoot. Check the Pods and the compute they run on. Choose E K S when you want Kubernetes without operating the control plane yourself.",
          "text_sha256": "0c1dafb805b7aec0c4ed05d22fcf3fd48dc784f96deb563c98092c6add9b4c6b",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "79a955c08692c8fc305592868547f2c4c165d1c4db6447d4fc835f3ab72f8b49",
          "asset_sha256": "cd3968bc49b0c0fe31a48ed372b7e08ca5701fd81bc3bd61cea1063306faefaa"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
